+// crosses two separately registered pool segments (165e: w3cldiag_xseg load=1 bytes=196608 split_at=131072).
+// The component cache joins host-VA-adjacent chunks into one transfer; CopySnapshot resolves the device alias of
+// the span's FIRST byte only and runs one kernel over the whole span. When the span continues into a second
+// registration whose alias is not contiguous with the first, the kernel reads (restore) or writes (capture) past
+// the first registration's alias: unmapped device VA = page fault = device_lost; mapped VA = wrong bytes.
+//   (A) GUFO_CACHE_SEG_GAP=1      CommitPages maps kGuardBytes more than it registers, so no two registered pool
+//                                 segments are adjacent in host VA and the join can never cross a registration.
+//   (C) GUFO_CACHE_ALIAS_CHECK=1  CopySnapshot also checks the alias of the span's last byte (and one byte every
+//                                 kProbeStride); a span that is not one linear alias is copied as one call per
+//                                 registration (same bytes, same destination, same stream order).
+// Unset or any other value: the production code path (A: mmap/munmap of exactly `bytes`; C: no extra query).
+// Each knob prints one event=w3segfix_config line when first read; with GUFO_CL_DIAG=1, every span C splits
+// prints event=w3segfix_alias_split with running totals. Read once per process. Pure C++ (no HIP types), so the
+// span logic is unit-tested on the CPU (xseg-fix/host-test).
+//
+// r2 (xseg-fix-r2): the R26-06 log extension (namespace r26 below), GUFO_CL_DIAG=1 ONLY. Unset, the
+// only addition to any path is one predictable `Get().diag` branch per cache copy / pool segment (never per token).
+//   w3segfix_reg / _unreg   pool registration table: segment id, host VA, bytes, guard, device alias
+//   w3segfix_devbuf         device allocation behind the device side of a cache copy (first sight; id, base, bytes)
+//   w3segfix_cross          a span whose host range crosses a pool registration (printed BEFORE it is submitted):
+//                           segment / offset / split point, alias-linear, and the class of the device VA the single
+//                           kernel would touch past the boundary: reg (pool registration j) / devbuf / mapped / unmapped
+//   w3segfix_copy           every cache copy after it is enqueued: cseq, thread, path, running counters
